# Drafted by Gemini (R25 closing), edited.

~ marianna_grey
Narrator: She smells of warm hay and spilled cider, twisting a length of braided horsehair around her thumb.
marianna_grey [smile]: You are still here. I kept looking toward the arch, thinking you might have gone without a word.
if flag.alda_will_delivered
marianna_grey [weary]: Alda left me the Green Queen Inn, all of it, in writing. I burn the toast and drop the tankards, yet folks keep coming in for the warmth.
endif
if not flag.alda_will_delivered
marianna_grey [sad]: They nailed planks across the Green Queen's doors yesterday. I still sleep up in the stable loft, where the horses breathe slow and nobody asks for ale.
endif
marianna_grey [smile]: Take this braid from Clover's tail, please, to keep in your pocket for luck on the road.
set ep_closing_marianna_grey
set ep_closings += 1
-> END

~ siv_harga
Narrator: Siv's apron is scrubbed clean, though the salt of old blood and fresh tears clings to her knuckles.
siv_harga [sad]: I washed the block and hung the cleavers high. Holly is gone into the cold dirt at the Rise, and the shop is too quiet for one pair of boots.
if flag.feast_winner == "siv"
siv_harga [weary]: When my sausage rolls won the feast, she laughed so hard she spilled her cider. I would trade the ribbon for one more scolding from her.
endif
if flag.feast_winner == "holly"
siv_harga [weary]: Her braided loaf won the feast, you know, golden and sweet as summer. She was proud of that crust, even with the rot in her heart.
endif
if flag.feast_winner == "both"
siv_harga [smile]: We shared the feast table, my meat and her bread side by side. That is how I will keep her in my head, not how she fell.
endif
siv_harga [neutral]: I still whisper a prayer when the blade comes down, for the beast, and for her foolish soul. Walk safe under the moon, friends.
set ep_closing_siv_harga
set ep_closings += 1
-> END

~ doris_squire
Narrator: Her lantern glass is cracked down the middle, and a folded scrap of ink-stained paper pokes from her cuff.
doris_squire [weary]: Still breathing. They took me early at the Rise, tied me tight, and Howie just stood there watching the rope bite.
if flag.mori_outcome == "doris"
doris_squire [neutral]: You brought Mori's word to me straight when you did not have to. I keep that in mind while I sort the keys.
endif
doris_squire [sad]: Watchhouse has got one chair left and one coat on the peg. I will walk the rounds alone till I find folk who do not turn in the dark.
doris_squire [neutral]: Made a rhyme about the moon last hour. Kept it to four lines. Goodnight to you.
set ep_closing_doris_squire
set ep_closings += 1
-> END

~ wendel_somerton
Narrator: Wendel clutches a sheaf of damp parchment to his waistcoat, his ink-horn clinking against his brass chain of office.
wendel_somerton [weary]: What a shambles, what an irregular, undocumented catastrophe for the parish register! Half the Round blames me for the weather, the weeds, and the late unpleasantness.
if flag.quarantine_forged
wendel_somerton [sly]: Someone forged my seal on a quarantine writ during the panic, a passable hand, though the loop on the W was vulgar. I shall not inquire further.
endif
if flag.golub_dead
wendel_somerton [smile]: At least the Hollow breathes easier without Golub rattling the fences, rest his terrible soul.
endif
wendel_somerton [neutral]: Inform the public that the Mayor's Office resumes regular hours from Twilight to Nightfall, pending form seven-B. You have my gratitude, officially and otherwise.
set ep_closing_wendel_somerton
set ep_closings += 1
-> END

~ jean_myriel
Narrator: Soot streaks his thick forearms, and the iron in his bucket still hisses steam into the damp night air.
jean_myriel [weary]: Holly used to bring me cold pies when the bellows broke. Hard to think a girl who baked like that wanted the world torn open.
jean_myriel [neutral]: Got iron grave-markers on the left of the anvil and cart-horse shoes on the right. The living and the dead both need nails, so I keep swinging.
jean_myriel [smile]: All that twisted cult scrap you lot shook loose makes fine hinges once you beat the malice out of it. Fare you well, travelers.
set ep_closing_jean_myriel
set ep_closings += 1
-> END

~ eleanor_heyling
Narrator: Three sleepy children cling to the hem of her dusty shawl while she marks her place in a folio with a dried dandelion.
eleanor_heyling [smile]: Oh, there you are, right on the closing page, much like the wandering knights in Holcroft's third appendix, footnote twelve.
if flag.price_outcome == "eleanor"
eleanor_heyling [smile]: The silver from that fair book-trade you brokered bought winter boots for all three little ones, with pennies left for tallow.
endif
if not flag.price_outcome
eleanor_heyling [neutral]: Before you vanish into the mist, do return my copy of "On Bog-Lights and Barrow-Wights," though I suppose I may have lent it to the baker.
endif
eleanor_heyling [weary]: The children asked if the moon would stop grinning now. I cited Peake on lunar phases, then simply held them until they slept.
set ep_closing_eleanor_heyling
set ep_closings += 1
-> END

~ finneas_trout
Narrator: Chalk dust powders his cuffs, and the smell of beeswax candles drifts from the small package under his arm.
finneas_trout [neutral]: I have been drafting a curriculum on recent municipal history for the youngsters. One must frame terror as a civic lesson, or they will never sleep.
if flag.thread_duality_catharsis == "taken"
finneas_trout [weary]: Since the shadow split from me, my own voice sounds quieter in the schoolroom. It is a relief, I think, to be only one man.
endif
if not flag.thread_duality_catharsis
finneas_trout [smile]: Oswick Frey gave me fresh tapers for the desks, and Oswick says the wax burns cleaner now. Did I mention Wick twice? Purely pedagogical.
endif
finneas_trout [smile]: Go with good grammar and stout hearts, my friends. The children will know your names.
set ep_closing_finneas_trout
set ep_closings += 1
-> END

~ sister_rain
Narrator: Her red hair shines like copper wire in the lantern light, and mud cakes the hem of her sunflower-stitched robe.
sister_rain [smile]: Look at the sky, dry as a bone, and yet I could weep buckets for joy and fury both! The Patient Lady keeps her garden even in the ash.
if flag.crow_outcome == "freed"
sister_rain [smile]: The Foxwillow crow-folk are loose in the high branches again, cawing their ragged throats raw, blessed little thieves every one.
endif
if flag.crow_outcome == "kept"
sister_rain [angry]: My heart still knots up thinking of those Foxwillow crows left in their cages. A caged wing is an insult to the rain.
endif
if flag.crow_outcome == "music"
sister_rain [weary]: Sometimes on the wind from Foxwillow I hear that strange crow-music rising, sweet and hollow as a reed pipe.
endif
sister_rain [smile]: Down at the edge of the burned Wytchwood, green shoots are pushing through the char already. Nothing stays dead here, least of all hope.
set ep_closing_sister_rain
set ep_closings += 1
-> END

~ weston_murdoch
Narrator: His voice carries over the murmur of the Round, warm as chestnuts roasting in a tin pail.
weston_murdoch [smile]: Bless your weary feet, travelers, for you walked through the furnace and brought back the dawn in your pockets.
if flag.bell_outcome == "restored"
weston_murdoch [smile]: Listen close and you can hear the monastery bell ringing clear across the moor, bronze and bright as a robin's throat.
endif
if flag.bell_outcome == "left"
weston_murdoch [weary]: The old bell hangs silent in its tower still, so I raise my own voice twice as loud to fill the hollow air.
endif
if flag.thread_rapture_catharsis == "taken"
weston_murdoch [sad]: So many of our little flock rose up in pale light and left their shoes behind. The benches are empty, yet the song remains.
endif
weston_murdoch [smile]: Keep a verse on your tongue when the track grows dark.
set ep_closing_weston_murdoch
set ep_closings += 1
-> END

~ solomon_roderick
Narrator: Marble dust sits white in the deep scars along his jaw, and little Sofia sleeps against his shoulder.
solomon_roderick [weary]: My mallet has not cooled since the shouting stopped. Every family wants good slate and deep letters so the rain will not wash the names away.
if flag.vigil_deaths >= 4
solomon_roderick [sad]: The new row in the yard stretches longer than the quarry can answer. My wrists ache with the weight of so many neighbors.
endif
solomon_roderick [smile]: Sofia drew four little figures in chalk on my workbench this hour. She says they are the ones who kept the roof from falling.
set ep_closing_solomon_roderick
set ep_closings += 1
-> END

~ mori_shade
Narrator: She smells of dried lavender and cold spade-iron, her pale hands folded neat as a closed book.
mori_shade [neutral]: The earth is tucked in tight tonight, travelers, and not a soul wanders without a stone to mark their pillow.
if flag.mori_outcome == "burn"
mori_shade [smile]: I burned those dreadful papers you brought me until the ash went up the chimney like grey moths.
endif
if flag.mori_outcome == "warn"
mori_shade [weary]: I took your warning to heart and bolted the lych-gate before the dark came down heavy.
endif
if flag.mori_outcome == "silent"
mori_shade [sly]: What you told me stays down in the clay with the roots, quiet as a coffin nail.
endif
if flag.mori_outcome == "doris"
mori_shade [neutral]: I carried the whole ugly truth straight to Constable Squire, just as we agreed.
endif
mori_shade [smile]: Every grave in my care has its proper name carved clean now. May your own rest be a long way off.
set ep_closing_mori_shade
set ep_closings += 1
-> END

~ morgan
Narrator: Their pockets clink with stoppered vials, and a smudge of blue ointment sits behind their left ear.
morgan [neutral]: I bottled three drams of moon-dew before I remembered the nightmare was over, or perhaps that was Tuesday.
if flag.quack_outcome == "exposed"
morgan [smile]: Since you ran that fraud Barnaby out of the square, folk actually drink my willow-bark draughts without asking for coloured sugar.
endif
if flag.quack_outcome == "left"
morgan [weary]: Barnaby packed his painted bottles and slipped off in the fog, leaving me three crates of corks that fit nothing.
endif
if flag.quack_outcome == "partner"
morgan [sad]: I am now legally "Morgan and Barnaby, Purveyors of Wonder," which is mortifying, as he puts cinnamon in the poultices.
endif
morgan [smile]: Sometimes when I smell bruised mint, I almost remember my mother's apron from before I woke here. Perhaps tomorrow it will come clear.
set ep_closing_morgan
set ep_closings += 1
-> END

~ geneva_fairchild
Narrator: Her leather mailbag hangs light at her hip, buckled with polished brass, her lilac eyes bright in the torchlight.
geneva_fairchild [smile]: The sorting racks are clean from top to bottom, and I am running the post office all by myself now.
if flag.letter_outcome == "seized"
geneva_fairchild [sad]: I still fret over that letter you seized from the bag, since a postmistress ought to deliver every word, even wicked ones.
endif
if flag.letter_outcome == "sent"
geneva_fairchild [weary]: I delivered that last terrible letter with my own hands, and the seal felt heavy as lead until it left my fingers.
endif
geneva_fairchild [smile]: If you ever write back from wherever the train takes you, put "Wickermoor Round" on the front and I will see it read.
set ep_closing_geneva_fairchild
set ep_closings += 1
-> END

~ jeremiah_stover
Narrator: Crumbled peat clings to his boots, and he tests a small, knobbed tuber with his thumbnail.
jeremiah_stover [neutral]: Ground took a beating from all that tramping and witch-fire, no two ways about it.
if flag.field_outcome == "truth"
jeremiah_stover [smile]: Foxwillow folk heard the straight truth of it, and the union ledger is squared up fair to the last copper.
endif
if flag.field_outcome == "lie"
jeremiah_stover [weary]: That lie we told at Foxwillow held fast enough, though it sits like a cold stone in my belly when I pass the stiles.
endif
if flag.field_outcome == "silence"
jeremiah_stover [neutral]: Nobody spoke a word about the Foxwillow business, and a quiet ditch keeps the sheep from straying, I reckon.
endif
jeremiah_stover [smile]: Yield is thin this digging, small roots and twisted skins, but the soil beneath is sweet again. Thank you for that.
set ep_closing_jeremiah_stover
set ep_closings += 1
-> END

~ hilda_brom
Narrator: The scent of pine resin and wet fur hangs where the shadow of the eaves meets the square.
if flag.thread_slaughter_catharsis == "taken"
Narrator: Hilda is gone to the deep wilds; pinned to the oak is acured winter wolf-pelt and a scrap of birchbark in her blunt hand.
hilda_brom [neutral]: "Gone where the tracks have no boots in them. Keep the pelt warm. The Queen knows your scent."
endif
if not flag.thread_slaughter_catharsis
hilda_brom [neutral]: Bow is unstrung. Snare-wire is coiled in the grease-pot.
hilda_brom [smile]: I sit on the black ridge and watch the fern-heads uncurl through the Wytchwood ash. The Green Queen breathes slow tonight, and so do I.
endif
set ep_closing_hilda_brom
set ep_closings += 1
-> END

~ thomasin_ardor
Narrator: Silver pins gleam along her lapel, and four folded coats of plum and midnight wool rest over her arm.
thomasin_ardor [smile]: Stand still a moment and let me look at your shoulders. You have torn every seam I mended, just as I expected.
if flag.curses_outcome == "apology"
thomasin_ardor [neutral]: Your apology stitched up an old rent in my pride, cleaner than silk thread.
endif
if flag.curses_outcome == "truth"
thomasin_ardor [weary]: The truth pricked like a bodkin when you spoke it, yet a clean cut heals straighter than a tear.
endif
if flag.curses_outcome == "warned"
thomasin_ardor [neutral]: You heeded my warning when the threads grew tangled, and that spared us all a darker shroud.
endif
if flag.curses_outcome == "unpicked"
thomasin_ardor [smile]: Watching that foul curse unpicked knot by knot was the finest needlework I have seen in Druskenvald.
endif
thomasin_ardor [smile]: I made a travelling coat for each of you, lined with satin and cut for high winds, no drab village grey about them.
set ep_closing_thomasin_ardor
set ep_closings += 1
-> END

~ beatrice_wells
Narrator: Sweet-smelling shavings of charred oak curl in her hair, and her cousins hum a low chord behind her.
beatrice_wells [smile]: Hear the saws go, high and low, raising up the Brightsinger roof where the fire left the sky bare!
beatrice_wells [neutral]: We hauled the black timber out of the burned wood, and the heartwood underneath is sound and golden as honey.
if flag.wicker_man_burned
beatrice_wells [smile]: We wove the charred ribs of that great wicker effigy right into the high rafters, so the terror holds up the song.
endif
beatrice_wells [smile]: "Nail the beam and plumb the wall, night is long and roofs are tall!" Go in tune, my darlings.
set ep_closing_beatrice_wells
set ep_closings += 1
-> END

~ mrs_oakum
Narrator: She stands half behind the water-butt, her shawl pulled low, smelling of wet wool and mountain sage.
mrs_oakum [weary]: Fair's too loud for my ears, all that fiddle-scrape and laughing. Came down only to see your faces before the mist takes you.
if flag.oakum_outcome == "help"
mrs_oakum [smile]: You held the chain and saw me through the bad moon without flinching. I never had friends who stayed till the fur shed.
endif
if flag.oakum_outcome == "kept"
mrs_oakum [neutral]: You kept your mouths shut about what I am, and that gave me breath enough to pack my bundle in peace.
endif
if flag.oakum_outcome == "told"
mrs_oakum [afraid]: Folk cross the street when my shadow falls now, since you told them what howls in me. I cannot bide where doors bolt at my step.
endif
mrs_oakum [neutral]: I am going quiet into the high hills where only the heather cares what teeth I carry. Fare well.
set ep_closing_mrs_oakum
set ep_closings += 1
-> END
